export const HE1={title:'The event and choosing a health topic',hook:'Health Education asks your team to do what health educators do every day: choose an important health topic, plan a lesson for a specific group of learners, teach it to a real audience and find out whether it worked. The event rewards lessons that are meaningful, engaging and carefully evaluated—and it starts with choosing the right topic.',objectives:['Describe the Health Education event, its rounds and scoring.','Classify topics into physical, mental, environmental and societal health.','Choose a topic that is meaningful for a specific audience.','Plan a year-long timeline for the project.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Health Education is a **team event for 2–6 members** in the middle school, secondary and postsecondary/collegiate divisions. The team selects a **health-related concept or instructional objective**, prepares a lesson for a **targeted group of learners**, **teaches it to a real audience** and **evaluates the results**.',
'**Round One** is a **pre-judged digital portfolio** of up to **20 pages** (not counting references) uploaded by one member as **one PDF** by **May 15**; it is worth **65 points**. Teams that upload receive a **Round Two** appointment: a **five-minute presentation** to judges worth **90 points**, for a total of **155**. Notecards are allowed (electronic notes are not shown), no other visual aids are allowed and every member must take an active role.',
'Business attire or the official HOSA uniform earns bonus points when **all** members are properly dressed.',
{t:'table',caption:'Event structure',head:['Part','What you do','Points'],rows:[['Round One','Pre-judged portfolio (≤20 pages + references)','65'],['Round Two','5-minute presentation to judges','90'],['Total','Portfolio + presentation','155']]}]],
['Four dimensions of health',[
'The topic must relate to **physical health** (the normal functioning of the body—such as diabetes, brain health or assistive technology), **mental health** (psychological and emotional well-being—such as addictive behavior, depression and anxiety, eating disorders or insomnia), **environmental health** (environmental factors affecting health—such as air or water pollution, climate change or clean energy) or **societal health** (non-medical factors affecting outcomes—such as poverty and hunger, education imbalance, work-life balance or digital health).',
'The Round Two “quality of the lesson” section checks that the lesson is health related, so connect your topic clearly to health outcomes. Many topics cross dimensions—for example, sleep affects both physical and mental health.',
'Your topic **may not be repeated** from previous years, so choose something new for your team.',
sort('Which dimension of health?',['Physical','Mental','Environmental','Societal'],[['Managing type 2 diabetes',0],['Coping with test anxiety',1],['Indoor air pollution',2],['Food insecurity',3],['Healthy sleep and the brain',0],['Screen time and social media stress',1],['Safe drinking water',2],['Access to health care in rural areas',3]])]],
['What makes a strong topic',[
'Judges score whether the lesson is **meaningful, relevant and important to the audience**—“vital for this audience to know.” A strong topic addresses a **real health need** of the learners: vaping prevention for middle schoolers, safe medication storage for parents of toddlers, fall prevention for older adults or stress management for college students.',
'A good topic is also **narrow enough** to teach well in one lesson. “Nutrition” is too broad; “Reading nutrition labels to choose lower-sugar drinks” is focused and teachable. Narrow topics make clear objectives and evaluation much easier.',
'Use data to show the need, such as national survey results or local health statistics.',
"A quick test is to finish the sentence “By the end of our lesson, these learners will be able to…” in one line. If the sentence needs several clauses, the topic is probably still too broad to teach and evaluate well.",
sort('Focused or too broad?',['Focused','Too broad'],[['Reading labels to choose lower-sugar drinks',0],['Nutrition',1],['Hands-only CPR for high school students',0],['Health',1],['Safe medication storage for parents of toddlers',0],['Mental health',1]])]],
['Choosing your audience first or topic first',[
'Some teams start with an **audience** they can reach—a local elementary class, a senior center or younger HOSA members—and choose a topic that meets that group’s needs. Others start with a **topic** they care about and find an audience who needs it. Either way, the topic and audience must fit each other.',
'The lesson must be delivered to a **real audience**, so confirm early that you can schedule time with that group. Consider the number of participants; the title page asks for the audience age or grade level and the number of participants.',
'Talk with teachers, community leaders or program coordinators about what their group needs most.',
"Larger teams have an advantage here: with up to six members, you can split into pairs to contact several possible hosts at once. Keep one shared list of contacts and responses so no host receives duplicate requests.",
order('Choosing a topic and audience',['Brainstorm topics in all four dimensions','Identify audiences your team can reach','Match topics to each audience’s real needs','Check that the topic is new for your team','Narrow the topic to one teachable lesson','Confirm a date with the audience'])]],
['How judges score',[
'The **portfolio** (65 points) scores the **title page**, **reference page(s)**, **spelling, grammar and neatness**, **formatting** and **page limit** (5 each), plus the **lesson plan narrative**, **lesson outline**, **materials** and **feedback tools** (10 each).',
'The **presentation** (90 points) scores **objectives and accomplishments** and **inclusion of media/software** (10 each), **delivery**—voice, stage presence, diction and team participation (5 each)—and **quality of the lesson**: health related, meaningful and relevant, engaging and interesting, appropriate to the audience, and creative and original (10 each).',
'Half of the presentation points judge the **lesson itself**, so the quality of what you actually taught matters most.',
flip('Scoring highlights',[['Portfolio','65 points'],['Presentation','90 points'],['Quality of the lesson','50 points across five items'],['Feedback tools','10 points; show audience feedback'],['Media/software','10 points for describing media used'],['Bonus','Proper dress for all members']])]],
['A year-long plan',[
'**Early fall:** form the team, choose the topic and audience and confirm a date. **Fall:** research the topic, write objectives and design the lesson, materials and evaluation tools. **Winter:** teach the lesson to the real audience, collect feedback and photos (with permission) and analyze results. **Early spring:** write and format the portfolio, proofread and upload it as one PDF well before **May 15**. **Spring:** rehearse the five-minute presentation.',
'Teaching earlier leaves time to revise and even teach again if something does not work. Keep all drafts, photos and feedback in a shared folder.',
'Check your state conference’s rules and deadlines; if your state uses the HOSA upload system, upload again for ILC.',
order('Health Education timeline',['Choose topic and audience','Research and write objectives','Design lesson, materials and evaluation','Teach the real audience','Analyze feedback and results','Write and upload the portfolio by May 15','Rehearse the presentation'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Choosing a topic',{a:{text:'Your team wants to teach “nutrition” to a fifth-grade class. (Scenario invented for practice.) What would improve the plan?',choices:[['Narrow it, such as choosing lower-sugar drinks by reading labels','b','Correct.',true],['Keep it broad to cover everything','b','A focused topic is easier to teach and evaluate.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you show the topic matters to this audience?',choices:[['Use data on children’s sugary drink intake and ask the teacher about class needs','c','Correct.',true],['Assume they need it','c','Show the need with evidence.',false]]},c:{text:'Your focused, relevant topic is ready for objective writing.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Teams of 2–6 choose a health topic, teach a real audience and evaluate results; Round One is a pre-judged portfolio (≤20 pages plus references, one PDF by May 15, 65 points); Round Two is a 5-minute presentation (90 points); total 155.','Topics must relate to physical, mental, environmental or societal health and may not repeat the team’s previous topics; choose a focused topic that meets a real need of the audience, supported by data.','Half of Round Two scores the lesson’s quality—health related, meaningful, engaging, audience-appropriate and creative—so plan early, teach early and leave time to revise.'],terms:[['Target audience','The specific group of learners the lesson is designed for.'],['Societal health','Non-medical factors such as poverty that affect health outcomes.'],['Environmental health','Environmental factors that affect human health and disease.']],mcqs:[q('How many members may be on a Health Education team?','2–6','1','2 only','8–10','Team size.'),q('How many pages may the portfolio have, not counting references?','20','10','30','16','Page limit.'),q('What is the total possible score?','155','100','225','65','65 + 90.'),q('Which is a societal health topic?','Food insecurity','Diabetes','Air pollution','Insomnia','Non-medical factor.'),q('Which topic is focused enough for one lesson?','Reading labels to choose lower-sugar drinks','Nutrition','Health','Wellness','Narrow.'),q('The lesson must be presented to:','A real audience','Only the judges','No one','A mirror','Rule.'),q('How long is the Round Two presentation?','5 minutes','10 minutes','3 minutes','15 minutes','Time limit.'),q('Which is true about topics?','They may not be repeated from previous years','Any old topic may be reused','They must be about nutrition','They must be environmental','Rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the Health Education event and how your team would choose a strong topic and audience.',rubric:[['Event',['2-6','portfolio','20 pages','pdf','may 15','5 minutes','real audience','155']],['Dimensions',['physical','mental','environmental','societal','health related']],['Strong topic',['meaningful','relevant','need','focused','narrow','data']],['Audience and planning',['audience','grade','participants','confirm','timeline','teach early']]],model:'Health Education is a team event for two to six members who choose a health topic, plan a lesson for a specific audience, teach it to a real group and evaluate the results. In Round One, judges score our pre-judged portfolio of up to 20 pages plus references, uploaded by one member as a single PDF by May 15, for 65 points; in Round Two, we give a five-minute presentation to judges worth 90 points, for a total of 155. Our topic must relate to physical, mental, environmental or societal health and must be new for our team. To choose well, we would brainstorm topics in all four dimensions and identify audiences we can actually reach, such as a fifth-grade class at a nearby elementary school. After talking with the teacher about the class’s needs, we might choose a focused topic like reading nutrition labels to choose lower-sugar drinks, rather than the broad topic of nutrition, because a narrow topic leads to clear objectives and measurable results. We would use data on children’s sugary drink intake to show why the topic is important for this audience, since judges score how meaningful and relevant the lesson is. Finally, we would confirm a teaching date early in the year so we have time to teach, collect feedback, analyze results and write the portfolio before the deadline.'},sources:[RULES,HP]};

export const HE2={title:'Knowing your audience',hook:'A lesson on vaping that captivates seventh graders might bore college students—and a lesson designed for adults might confuse fourth graders. Judges score how appropriate your instruction is for the targeted audience and how much thought went into making it well received. Great health educators start by understanding their learners.',objectives:['Describe learner characteristics at different ages and stages.','Conduct a simple needs assessment for an audience.','Adapt content, vocabulary and activities to the audience.','Plan for health literacy, culture and accessibility.'],sections:[
['Learners at different ages',[
'Learners differ in how they think and learn. **Young children** (about ages 5–8) learn best through concrete, hands-on activities, stories, songs and short segments; their attention spans are short. **Older children** (about 9–12) can handle more facts and simple reasoning and enjoy games and competitions. **Adolescents** value peer opinions, independence and relevance to their lives, and respond to real-life scenarios and discussion.',
'**Adults** bring experience and want practical information they can use immediately; they appreciate respect, choice and problem-centered learning. **Older adults** may benefit from larger print, slower pacing and attention to hearing or vision needs, while drawing on rich life experience.',
'Developmental theories, such as Piaget’s stages of cognitive development, help explain why concrete examples work well for young learners and abstract reasoning becomes possible in adolescence.',
"Within any age group, learners still vary widely. Some students read well above grade level while others are learning English, so plan activities that let every learner participate, such as picture cues, partner work and hands-on tasks.",
match('Audience and effective strategy',[['First graders','Songs, stories and hands-on demos'],['Fifth graders','Games and simple challenges'],['Middle schoolers','Peer-relevant scenarios and discussion'],['High school students','Real-life decisions and debates'],['Adults','Practical, problem-centered information'],['Older adults','Large print, slower pace, experience-based discussion']])]],
['Needs assessment',[
'A **needs assessment** identifies what your audience already knows, what they need to learn and what matters to them. Methods include a short **pre-survey**, a conversation with the teacher or program leader, local **health data** and observation.',
'For example, before teaching hand hygiene to second graders, you might ask the teacher which habits the class struggles with and give a quick picture-based pre-quiz. Before teaching stress management to college students, you might survey their main sources of stress.',
'Needs assessment results belong in your lesson plan narrative as evidence of planning.',
"Keep needs assessment tools very short, often three to five questions, so they do not use up teaching time. The goal is to learn enough to aim your lesson, not to collect detailed personal information.",
order('A simple needs assessment',['Talk with the group’s leader or teacher','Review relevant health data','Give a short pre-survey or pre-quiz','Identify knowledge gaps and interests','Set objectives that address the gaps'])]],
['Adapting content and vocabulary',[
'Match vocabulary to your learners. Use **plain language**—short sentences, common words and active voice—and define any necessary medical terms. For a third-grade class, “germs” works better than “pathogens”; for nursing students, precise terminology is expected.',
'Adjust **depth and pace**: fewer key messages for younger audiences, more detail and nuance for older ones. Limit the lesson to about **three key messages** that learners should remember.',
'Test your materials with someone from the audience age group if possible.',
"Readability tools built into many word processors can estimate the grade level of your handouts. For general audiences, many health communicators aim for about a sixth- to eighth-grade reading level.",
sort('Good fit for a third-grade audience?',['Good fit','Poor fit'],[['“Germs can make us sick, so we wash our hands.”',0],['“Pathogenic microorganisms are transmitted via fomites.”',1],['A 20-second handwashing song',0],['A 30-minute lecture with no breaks',1],['Glitter “germ” demonstration',0],['Statistics tables with percentages',1]])]],
['Health literacy',[
'**Health literacy** is the degree to which people can find, understand and use health information to make decisions. Many adults have limited health literacy, and anyone can struggle with complex information when stressed or unfamiliar with a topic.',
'Good health education uses **plain language**, **visuals**, **demonstrations**, **chunking** (small pieces of information) and **teach-back** (asking learners to explain or show what they learned in their own words). These techniques help every learner, not just those with limited literacy.',
'Describing health literacy strategies in your portfolio shows judges thoughtful planning.',
chk('What is teach-back?',['Asking learners to explain or demonstrate what they learned in their own words','Teaching the lesson backward','Reading the slides aloud twice','Giving a test with no feedback'],'Checks understanding.')]],
['Culture, inclusion and accessibility',[
'Respect learners’ **cultures, languages and experiences**. Use examples, foods, names and images that reflect the audience, and avoid stereotypes. For a multilingual audience, consider translated handouts or bilingual team members.',
'Plan for **accessibility**: readable fonts and contrast, captions on videos, seating that lets everyone see and hear, and activities that people with different physical abilities can join. Be sensitive with personal topics such as weight, mental health or family situations—focus on behaviors and resources, not judgment.',
'An inclusive lesson is more meaningful and engaging for everyone.',
"If your topic involves sensitive issues, ask the host in advance whether any learners have experiences that may make the content difficult, and plan a way for students to step out or talk privately with a trusted adult.",
sort('Inclusive practice?',['Inclusive','Not inclusive'],[['Using foods familiar to the audience in nutrition examples',0],['Jokes based on stereotypes',1],['Captioning a video',0],['Singling out a student’s weight',1],['Offering a translated handout',0]])]],
['Working with the host',[
'Your audience usually comes through a **host**—a teacher, coach, youth leader or facility coordinator. Contact them early, explain the HOSA project, agree on the date, time, room, number of participants and any technology, and ask about rules for photos and surveys.',
'For minors, follow school policies on **permission** for photographs and surveys; never collect personal identifying information unnecessarily. Send the host your lesson plan in advance and a thank-you note afterward.',
'A good relationship with the host may let you teach again or reach new groups.',
ex('Planning with a host','Draft key details to confirm with an elementary teacher (details invented for practice).',['Date and time: Tuesday, 1:15–1:45 p.m.','Audience: 24 fourth graders','Room: classroom with projector','Photos: only with school permission; no faces without consent','Survey: anonymous, picture-based'],'A confirmed plan avoids surprises on teaching day (details invented for practice).')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Adapting a lesson',{a:{text:'Your sleep lesson was written for high school students, but your host offers a group of third graders instead. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Redesign with simple words, a story and an active sleep-routine game','b','Correct.',true],['Use the same slides and statistics','b','Content must fit the audience.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you check what the students already know?',choices:[['A short picture-based pre-quiz and a conversation with the teacher','c','Correct.',true],['Skip it','c','Needs assessment guides the lesson.',false]]},c:{text:'Your revised lesson is age-appropriate and ready to teach.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Learners differ by age: concrete, hands-on activities for young children; games for older children; relevant scenarios and peer discussion for adolescents; practical, respectful problem-solving for adults; accessible pacing for older adults.','Use a needs assessment (host conversation, data, pre-survey) to find gaps; adapt vocabulary, depth and pace; limit key messages; apply health literacy strategies like plain language, visuals, chunking and teach-back.','Plan for culture, inclusion and accessibility; work professionally with the host, follow permission policies for photos and surveys and avoid collecting identifying information.'],terms:[['Needs assessment','Finding out what learners know, need and value before teaching.'],['Health literacy','The ability to find, understand and use health information.'],['Teach-back','Asking learners to explain or show what they learned.']],mcqs:[q('Which strategy best fits first graders?','Songs, stories and hands-on demos','Long lectures','Statistics tables','Research papers','Concrete learning.'),q('Adults generally prefer:','Practical, problem-centered information','Songs and puppets','No examples','Memorizing lists','Adult learning.'),q('A needs assessment identifies:','What learners know and need','The judges’ names','The cost of supplies','The weather','Planning.'),q('Which is plain language?','“Germs can make us sick.”','“Pathogenic microorganisms proliferate.”','“Utilize fomite mitigation.”','“Transmission vectors abound.”','Simple words.'),q('Teach-back means:','Learners explain or show what they learned','The teacher repeats the lesson','Students teach the judges','Reading slides backward','Checks understanding.'),q('Which is an inclusive practice?','Captioning videos','Stereotype jokes','Singling out students','Ignoring language needs','Accessibility.'),q('How many key messages should a lesson usually focus on?','About three','About twenty','None','Fifty','Memorable.'),q('For surveys and photos with minors, you should:','Follow school permission policies','Collect full names and addresses','Post photos freely','Skip asking','Privacy.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would learn about and adapt a lesson to a specific target audience.',rubric:[['Learner characteristics',['age','children','adolescents','adults','hands-on','attention','developmental']],['Needs assessment',['needs assessment','pre-survey','teacher','data','gaps']],['Adaptation and literacy',['plain language','vocabulary','three key messages','visuals','chunking','teach-back']],['Inclusion and host',['culture','accessibility','captions','host','permission','privacy']]],model:'Our audience would be 24 seventh graders at a local middle school learning about the risks of vaping. Seventh graders are early adolescents who value peer opinions, independence and relevance to their lives, so we would use realistic peer scenarios, discussion and a competitive team game rather than a lecture. First, we would conduct a needs assessment: we would talk with the health teacher about what students already know and what they see at school, review youth tobacco survey data showing how common e-cigarette use is among middle schoolers and give a short anonymous pre-survey asking about common myths. The results would guide our objectives. We would use plain language and define terms like nicotine dependence simply, focus on three key messages—vapes contain nicotine, nicotine affects the developing brain and there are ways to refuse—and use visuals, short chunks of information and teach-back by asking students to role-play refusing a vape in their own words. To be inclusive, we would choose diverse names and examples in scenarios, caption any video, use high-contrast slides and avoid shaming anyone who may already vape, instead sharing resources for quitting. We would work with the teacher on the date, room, technology and school policies for anonymous surveys and photos, avoid collecting any identifying information and send a thank-you note afterward.'},sources:[RULES,LIFE,LIT]};

export const HE3={title:'Writing goals and objectives',hook:'“Teach kids about germs” is an idea, not a lesson plan. Judges look for a clear goal and objectives that are detailed and measurable—and in Round Two they score how well you highlight your objectives and accomplishments. Well-written objectives guide every activity you plan and every question on your evaluation.',objectives:['Distinguish a lesson goal from learning objectives.','Write measurable objectives using action verbs and the ABCD or SMART approach.','Use Bloom’s taxonomy to target different levels of thinking.','Align objectives with activities and evaluation.'],sections:[
['Goals versus objectives',[
'A **goal** is a broad statement of what the lesson aims to achieve: “Students will understand how to prevent the spread of germs.” **Objectives** are specific, measurable statements of what learners will be able to do by the end: “Students will demonstrate the six steps of handwashing for at least 20 seconds.”',
'A typical lesson has **one goal** and **two to four objectives**. Each objective should be something you can **observe or measure**, so your evaluation can show whether it was achieved.',
'The lesson plan narrative should state the goal and objectives clearly near the beginning.',
"Writing the goal first keeps the team focused on the big picture, while objectives break that picture into steps you can teach in the time available. If an activity does not help reach any objective, consider cutting it.",
sort('Goal or objective?',['Goal','Objective'],[['Students will understand healthy sleep',0],['Students will list three habits that improve sleep',1],['Parents will learn about medication safety',0],['Parents will identify two safe places to store medicines',1],['Teens will value mental health',0],['Teens will name two trusted adults they can talk to',1]])]],
['Measurable action verbs',[
'Avoid vague verbs such as **understand**, **know**, **learn** or **appreciate** in objectives, because you cannot directly observe them. Use **action verbs** that describe observable behavior: **list**, **identify**, **describe**, **demonstrate**, **explain**, **compare**, **choose**, **create**, **plan**.',
'For example, change “Students will know about nutrition labels” to “Students will identify the grams of added sugar on a nutrition label.” The revised objective tells you exactly what to teach and how to check it.',
'Action verbs also make your accomplishments easy to describe to judges.',
"Some verbs sit on the border. “Explain” is measurable if learners explain aloud or in writing; “recognize” is measurable if they pick the correct answer from options. Always ask, “How would I see or hear this?”",
sort('Measurable verb?',['Measurable','Not measurable'],[['Demonstrate',0],['Understand',1],['List',0],['Appreciate',1],['Compare',0],['Know',1]])]],
['The ABCD and SMART approaches',[
'The **ABCD** format includes **Audience** (who), **Behavior** (what they will do), **Condition** (under what circumstances) and **Degree** (how well). Example: “After the lesson (C), fourth graders (A) will identify healthy drink choices (B) in at least four of five pictures (D).”',
'The **SMART** approach checks that objectives are **Specific**, **Measurable**, **Achievable**, **Relevant** and **Time-bound**. Both approaches lead to objectives that can be evaluated.',
'Make sure objectives are **achievable in the time available**—a 30-minute lesson cannot change long-term habits, but it can build knowledge, skills and intentions.',
"Share your draft objectives with the host. Teachers often know whether an objective is realistic for their students and may suggest links to what the class is already studying.",
ex('Writing an ABCD objective','Turn “Teach CPR” into a measurable objective for high school students.',['Audience: high school students','Behavior: perform hands-only CPR compressions','Condition: on a manikin after a demonstration','Degree: at a rate of 100–120 per minute for one minute'],'After a demonstration, high school students will perform hands-only CPR compressions on a manikin at 100–120 per minute for one minute.')]],
['Bloom’s taxonomy',[
'**Bloom’s taxonomy** (revised) arranges thinking skills from lower to higher: **remember**, **understand**, **apply**, **analyze**, **evaluate** and **create**. Each level has typical verbs: list (remember), explain (understand), demonstrate (apply), compare (analyze), judge (evaluate), design (create).',
'Strong lessons move beyond remembering facts to applying and creating—for example, having learners plan a healthy snack or create a stress-management plan. Choose levels appropriate to the audience’s age.',
'Including higher-level objectives helps make a lesson “engaging” and “authentic.”',
"Younger audiences usually work mostly at the remember, understand and apply levels, while high school and college learners can analyze, evaluate and create. A single lesson can still include one higher-level task for any age, such as designing a simple plan.",
match('Bloom’s level and verb',[['Remember','List'],['Understand','Explain'],['Apply','Demonstrate'],['Analyze','Compare'],['Evaluate','Judge'],['Create','Design']])]],
['Domains of learning',[
'Objectives can target three **domains**. The **cognitive** domain covers knowledge and thinking (“identify the signs of a stroke”). The **affective** domain covers attitudes, values and confidence (“express confidence in refusing a vape”). The **psychomotor** domain covers physical skills (“demonstrate proper inhaler technique”).',
'Health behavior change often needs all three: knowledge of why, confidence and motivation, and the skill to act. Including at least one skill-based objective makes a lesson practical.',
'Affective objectives can be measured with confidence ratings or intention questions on surveys.',
"Health education research suggests that knowledge alone rarely changes behavior. Building skills and confidence—sometimes called self-efficacy—and helping learners plan specific actions makes healthy choices more likely after the lesson ends.",
sort('Which domain?',['Cognitive','Affective','Psychomotor'],[['List three signs of a stroke',0],['Rate confidence in calling 911',1],['Demonstrate handwashing steps',2],['Explain why sleep matters',0],['Express willingness to talk to a trusted adult',1],['Apply a bandage correctly',2]])]],
['Aligning objectives, activities and evaluation',[
'Every objective should connect to at least one **activity** that teaches it and at least one **evaluation item** that measures it. An **alignment table** makes this clear for you and for judges.',
'If an objective has no matching activity, learners may not achieve it; if it has no evaluation item, you cannot show it was achieved. Revise until every row is complete.',
{t:'table',caption:'Alignment table (example invented for practice)',head:['Objective','Activity','Evaluation'],rows:[['Identify added sugar on a label','Label scavenger hunt','Post-quiz picture item'],['Compare two drinks by sugar content','Sugar-cube demonstration','Choose the lower-sugar drink'],['Plan a healthy drink swap','Personal swap card','Collect completed cards']]}]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Fixing objectives',{a:{text:'Your draft objective reads, “Students will understand stress.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the problem?',choices:[['“Understand” is not measurable; use an action verb such as list or demonstrate','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing','b','You cannot observe understanding directly.',false]]},b:{text:'You rewrite it: “Students will demonstrate one breathing technique to reduce stress.” How will you evaluate it?',choices:[['Observe each group demonstrating the technique and record it on a checklist','c','Correct.',true],['Assume they learned it','c','Measure what you planned.',false]]},c:{text:'Your objectives, activities and evaluation now align.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['A goal is broad; objectives are specific and measurable—usually one goal with two to four objectives that you can observe or measure.','Use action verbs (list, identify, demonstrate, compare, create) rather than understand or know; write objectives with ABCD (audience, behavior, condition, degree) or SMART criteria, achievable in the time available.','Target Bloom’s levels from remember to create and the cognitive, affective and psychomotor domains; align every objective with an activity and an evaluation item.'],terms:[['Learning objective','A specific, measurable statement of what learners will be able to do.'],['Bloom’s taxonomy','A hierarchy of thinking skills from remembering to creating.'],['Psychomotor domain','Learning that involves physical skills.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a measurable objective?','Students will list three habits that improve sleep','Students will understand sleep','Students will appreciate sleep','Students will know about sleep','Action verb.'),q('Which verb is NOT measurable?','Understand','Demonstrate','List','Compare','Observable.'),q('In ABCD, the “D” stands for:','Degree','Date','Domain','Detail','How well.'),q('Which is the highest level of the revised Bloom’s taxonomy?','Create','Remember','Apply','Understand','Top level.'),q('Which domain covers physical skills?','Psychomotor','Cognitive','Affective','Social','Skills.'),q('Rating confidence in calling 911 targets which domain?','Affective','Psychomotor','Cognitive','None','Attitudes.'),q('What does an alignment table connect?','Objectives, activities and evaluation','Names and phone numbers','Fonts and colors','Judges and rooms','Planning.'),q('A goal is:','A broad statement of what the lesson aims to achieve','A test question','A single activity','A survey result','Broad.')],frq:{prompt:'Write a goal and three measurable objectives for a health lesson and explain how they align with activities and evaluation.',rubric:[['Goal and objectives',['goal','objectives','measurable','specific','action verb']],['Format',['abcd','smart','audience','behavior','condition','degree']],['Levels and domains',['bloom','apply','create','cognitive','affective','psychomotor']],['Alignment',['activity','evaluation','alignment','post-quiz','checklist','survey']]],model:'For a lesson on choosing lower-sugar drinks with fifth graders, our goal would be: Students will make healthier drink choices by understanding added sugar. Our three objectives would be measurable and use action verbs. First, after a label scavenger hunt, students will identify the grams of added sugar on a nutrition facts label with at least four of five labels correct; this cognitive objective at the remember and apply levels would be measured by a picture-based post-quiz. Second, after a sugar-cube demonstration, students will compare two drinks and choose the one with less added sugar; this analyze-level objective would be measured with paired pictures on the post-quiz. Third, by the end of the lesson, students will create a personal drink-swap plan naming one sugary drink they will replace with water or milk; this create-level objective also reaches the affective domain, and we would collect the completed swap cards and ask students to rate their confidence in making the swap. Each objective follows the ABCD format by naming the audience, behavior, condition and degree, and each is achievable within a 30-minute lesson. In our portfolio, an alignment table would show every objective with its matching activity and evaluation item, so judges can see exactly how we planned the lesson and how we measured our accomplishments.'},sources:[RULES,BLOOM]};

export const HE4={title:'Researching accurate health content',hook:'The lesson plan narrative must include data, research and supportive information about your topic—and everything you teach must be accurate. Health misinformation spreads quickly, and health educators carry responsibility for getting facts right. This lesson covers finding reliable sources, using data to show need and translating research into learner-friendly content.',objectives:['Identify reliable sources of health information and data.','Use data to show why a topic matters.','Evaluate sources for credibility and currency.','Translate research into accurate, plain-language key messages.'],sections:[
['Reliable sources',[
'Strong health education draws on **government health agencies** (CDC, NIH, MedlinePlus, FDA, state and local health departments), **international organizations** (WHO), **professional organizations** (American Academy of Pediatrics, American Heart Association), **peer-reviewed research** and **reputable university and hospital sources**.',
'National objectives such as **Healthy People 2030** describe priority health issues and targets, which can help justify your topic. National surveys—such as the Youth Risk Behavior Survey or National Youth Tobacco Survey—provide data on behaviors among young people.',
'Record each source’s author, date, title and URL for your APA reference page.',
"Fact sheets from these agencies are usually written for the public and are easier to translate for learners than technical reports. For data, look for the agency’s data briefs, dashboards or survey summaries, which present key numbers clearly.",
match('Source and use',[['CDC','Disease prevention data and guidance'],['MedlinePlus','Plain-language health information'],['Healthy People 2030','National health objectives and targets'],['WHO','Global health data and guidance'],['Peer-reviewed journal','Original research findings'],['Local health department','Community health data']])]],
['Using data to show need',[
'Judges score how meaningful and relevant the lesson is to the audience. **Data** show the need: how many people are affected, which groups are at higher risk and what difference prevention can make. For example, data on unintentional poisonings among young children support a medication-storage lesson for parents.',
'Choose a few clear statistics rather than many. Explain them in plain words and connect them to the audience: “Many middle schoolers have tried e-cigarettes, and most started with flavored products.”',
'Always cite the source and year, and avoid exaggerating numbers.',
"Local data can be especially persuasive. If your county health department reports higher rates of a problem than the state average, or your school’s own survey shows a need, mention it to connect the topic directly to your audience.",
chk('Which best justifies a lesson on medication storage for parents of toddlers?',['Data on unintentional poisonings in young children from a credible agency','A personal opinion','A random social media post','A guess about how common poisonings are'],'Credible data show need.')]],
['Evaluating credibility',[
'Evaluate sources with questions such as: **Who** wrote it, and what are their credentials? **What** evidence supports the claims? **When** was it published or updated? **Why** was it created—to inform, or to sell? Is the information **consistent** with other reliable sources?',
'Be cautious with commercial websites selling products, personal blogs, social media posts and sources that make dramatic claims without evidence. Even reliable sources can become outdated as guidelines change.',
'Cross-check key facts with at least two reliable sources.',
"For peer-reviewed research, read the abstract and conclusions carefully and note limitations such as small samples or different populations. A single study rarely settles a question; guidance from major health agencies usually reflects many studies.",
sort('Credible source?',['Credible','Questionable'],[['CDC fact sheet updated recently',0],['A supplement company’s product page',1],['Peer-reviewed study in a medical journal',0],['A viral video with no sources',1],['MedlinePlus health topic',0],['A ten-year-old blog post',1]])]],
['From research to key messages',[
'Research is often complex; your job is to translate it into **accurate, simple key messages**. A key message is a short statement learners should remember and act on: “Store medicines up and away, out of sight.” Limit the lesson to about **three key messages**, each supported by evidence.',
'Use **plain language**, examples and visuals to explain why each message matters. Avoid oversimplifying in ways that make statements false—for example, “all fats are bad” is inaccurate; “choose foods with less saturated fat” is more precise.',
'Have an advisor or health professional review your content for accuracy.',
"Write each key message as a positive action whenever possible—“Wash your hands for 20 seconds” rather than “Don’t forget to wash.” Positive, specific actions are easier for learners to remember and follow.",
order('Translating research',['Read reliable sources','Identify the most important findings','Write three key messages','Check accuracy with a second source','Simplify language for the audience','Ask an expert to review'])]],
['Avoiding misinformation and fear',[
'Health education should **inform and empower**, not frighten. Fear-based messages without practical steps can backfire, especially with young people. Pair information about risks with **specific actions** learners can take and **resources** they can use.',
'Correct common **myths** carefully: state the fact first, explain briefly why the myth is wrong and repeat the fact. Avoid repeating myths so often that learners remember them instead of the truth.',
'Respect privacy and avoid stigma, especially on topics such as mental health, substance use and weight.',
"Include at least one resource learners can use after the lesson, such as a hotline, a website or a trusted adult at school. Resources turn awareness into action and are especially important for mental health and substance use topics.",
sort('Empowering or fear-based?',['Empowering','Fear-based'],[['Showing three ways to refuse a vape',0],['Graphic images with no guidance',1],['Sharing a crisis line and trusted adults',0],['Telling teens they will definitely die',1],['Practicing a coping skill',0]])]],
['Writing the research section',[
'In the **lesson plan narrative**, summarize the research that supports your topic: the health issue, who is affected, key data, why the audience needs the lesson and what evidence-based strategies you used. Cite sources in text and list them on the reference page in **APA** format.',
'Keep the summary focused—one to two pages in a 20-page portfolio is often enough—so there is room for the lesson outline, materials and feedback tools.',
ex('A research paragraph','Draft a short justification for a sleep lesson for high school students (statistics invented for practice).',['State the issue: many teens get less sleep than recommended','Give data: a survey found 7 in 10 students slept under 8 hours on school nights','Explain effects: attention, mood and safety','Connect to the audience: our school starts early','Cite each source in APA style'],'A concise, cited justification shows judges why the lesson matters (statistics invented for practice).')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Checking content',{a:{text:'A teammate found a website claiming a supplement “cures anxiety in a week.” (Scenario invented for practice.) Should you use it?',choices:[['No—it is commercial and unsupported; use NIH or CDC information instead','b','Correct.',true],['Yes, it sounds helpful','b','Check credibility first.',false]]},b:{text:'Your mental health lesson includes a frightening statistic. How do you present it?',choices:[['Pair it with coping skills and resources such as trusted adults and crisis lines','c','Correct.',true],['Repeat it several times for impact','c','Empower, do not frighten.',false]]},c:{text:'Your content is accurate, cited and empowering.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Use reliable sources—CDC, NIH, MedlinePlus, WHO, Healthy People 2030, professional organizations, peer-reviewed research and local health departments—and record them for APA references.','Use a few clear, cited statistics to show the audience’s need; evaluate credibility with who, what, when and why; cross-check facts with two sources.','Translate research into about three accurate, plain-language key messages; pair risks with actions and resources, correct myths carefully and avoid stigma; summarize research concisely in the narrative.'],terms:[['Key message','A short, accurate statement learners should remember and act on.'],['Peer-reviewed','Evaluated by experts before publication.'],['Misinformation','False or inaccurate information.']],mcqs:[q('Which source provides plain-language health information?','MedlinePlus','A supplement ad','A celebrity post','An anonymous forum','NIH library.'),q('Which document sets national health objectives?','Healthy People 2030','A store flyer','A school menu','A movie script','ODPHP.'),q('Why include data in the narrative?','To show the audience’s need','To fill pages','To confuse judges','To replace objectives','Justification.'),q('Which is a warning sign of an unreliable source?','It sells a product with dramatic claims','It is from the CDC','It cites research','It was recently updated','Bias.'),q('How many key messages should a lesson usually have?','About three','About fifteen','None','One hundred','Focus.'),q('When correcting a myth, you should:','State the fact first and repeat it','Repeat the myth many times','Ignore it','Mock the learners','Myth correction.'),q('Fear-based messages work best when:','Paired with specific actions and resources','Used alone','Graphic and shocking','Repeated without guidance','Empowerment.'),q('Key facts should be cross-checked with:','At least two reliable sources','No sources','One blog','A friend’s opinion','Accuracy.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would research a health topic and turn the research into accurate, audience-appropriate lesson content.',rubric:[['Sources',['cdc','nih','medlineplus','who','healthy people','peer-reviewed','health department']],['Need and data',['data','statistics','need','audience','cite','year']],['Credibility',['credible','who','when','why','cross-check','commercial']],['Key messages',['key messages','three','plain language','accurate','myths','resources','expert']]],model:'For a lesson on safe medication storage for parents of toddlers, we would start with reliable sources: CDC data and guidance on unintentional poisonings in young children, MedlinePlus and FDA information on safe storage and disposal, the American Academy of Pediatrics’ advice for parents and our local health department’s data, and we would review Healthy People 2030 objectives related to injury prevention. We would use a few clear, cited statistics in our narrative to show why the topic matters to parents of young children, explaining them in plain words and noting the source and year. To evaluate credibility, we would ask who created each source, what evidence it provides, when it was updated and whether it is trying to sell something, and we would cross-check key facts with at least two reliable sources. Then we would translate the research into three key messages: store medicines up, away and out of sight; keep medicines in their original child-resistant containers and close them every time; and save the Poison Help number, 1-800-222-1222, in your phone. We would correct the myth that child-resistant caps are childproof by stating the fact first. We would pair risks with practical steps rather than using fear, ask our advisor and a pharmacist to review the content for accuracy and summarize our research in about a page of the narrative with APA citations.'},sources:[RULES,HP,PLAIN]};

export const HE5={title:'Designing a timed lesson',hook:'Judges use your lesson outline to evaluate the opening, organization, delivery and close of your instruction. A timed outline shows that you planned every minute—how you will hook learners, teach and practice key messages, check understanding and end with a memorable close. Good design is what makes a lesson engaging.',objectives:['Structure a lesson with an opening, body and close.','Use active learning strategies to engage learners.','Write a timed lesson outline.','Plan transitions, roles and backup activities.'],sections:[
['The structure of a lesson',[
'Most effective lessons share a structure. The **opening** (hook) captures attention and introduces the goal—a question, story, demonstration or surprising fact. The **body** teaches the key messages through explanation, demonstration and practice. A **check for understanding** confirms learning. The **close** summarizes key messages, encourages action and includes evaluation.',
'Models such as the **5E** model (Engage, Explore, Explain, Elaborate, Evaluate) or **Gagné’s events of instruction** give similar sequences that move from attention to practice to assessment.',
'Your timed outline should label each of these parts clearly.',
"Whichever model you choose, name it in your lesson plan narrative and label the outline’s segments with its stages. This shows judges that the lesson was designed intentionally rather than assembled from unrelated activities.",
order('5E lesson model',['Engage','Explore','Explain','Elaborate','Evaluate'])]],
['Opening strong',[
'The first few minutes set the tone. Effective hooks include a **question** (“How many teaspoons of sugar do you think are in this drink?”), a short **story**, a **demonstration** (glitter “germs” spreading on hands), a **quick poll** or a **mystery object**.',
'Then introduce the team, state the lesson’s purpose in learner-friendly words and, for older audiences, share the objectives. A **pre-test** can be given here if you are measuring change.',
'Keep the opening short—about 10% of the lesson time.',
"Practice the hook more than any other part. A confident, well-timed first minute earns learners’ attention, calms presenters’ nerves and sets up the rest of the lesson.",
sort('Strong hook or weak opening?',['Strong hook','Weak opening'],[['Guessing the sugar in a soda bottle',0],['Reading the title slide aloud',1],['Glitter germ handshake demonstration',0],['Apologizing for being nervous',1],['A quick hands-up poll about sleep',0]])]],
['Active learning strategies',[
'Learners remember more when they **do** something. Active strategies include **demonstrations with practice**, **games and quizzes**, **role-plays**, **case scenarios**, **small-group discussions**, **think-pair-share**, **hands-on stations** and **creating a product** such as a poster, plan or pledge.',
'Mix strategies to keep attention, especially with younger learners—change activities about every **5–10 minutes**. Make sure activities directly teach the objectives rather than simply filling time.',
'Engaging, interesting lessons earn up to 10 points in Round Two.',
"Plan how you will form groups and move between activities. Transitions can waste several minutes, so give clear instructions, have materials ready at each station and assign a team member to each group before the lesson begins.",
match('Strategy and example',[['Demonstration with practice','Students practice handwashing steps'],['Role-play','Practicing refusal skills for vaping'],['Think-pair-share','Partners discuss stress triggers'],['Game','Healthy-choice relay race'],['Case scenario','Deciding what to do for a choking classmate'],['Product creation','Designing a personal sleep plan']])]],
['The timed outline',[
'A **timed outline** lists each segment of the lesson with its **time**, **activity**, **presenter** and **materials**. It shows judges the lesson format and helps you stay on schedule. Add brief notes about how each segment connects to objectives.',
'Plan realistic times; activities often take longer than expected, especially with large or young groups.',
"Include a column for the objective each segment supports, or mark objectives with numbers. Judges can then see at a glance that every part of the lesson has a purpose and that the timing is realistic.",
{t:'table',caption:'Timed outline for a 30-minute lesson (invented for practice)',head:['Time','Segment','Presenter','Materials'],rows:[['0:00–3:00','Hook: guess the sugar','Ava','Soda bottle, sugar cubes'],['3:00–5:00','Pre-quiz','Ben','Picture quizzes'],['5:00–13:00','Reading labels demo and practice','Ava and Chen','Label cards'],['13:00–21:00','Label scavenger hunt game','All','Drink cartons'],['21:00–26:00','Drink-swap plan','Chen','Swap cards'],['26:00–30:00','Review, post-quiz and close','Ben','Post-quiz']]}]],
['Closing and calls to action',[
'A strong close **summarizes** the key messages, invites learners to **commit to an action** (a pledge, a plan or a take-home card) and completes **evaluation** such as a post-quiz or survey. End on an upbeat, memorable note—a slogan, a song or a quick recap game.',
'Give learners something to take home if appropriate, such as a handout for families. Thank the audience and the host.',
'Judges look for clear evidence of a strong opening, delivery and closing.',
"Leave a few minutes for the evaluation itself. Post-quizzes and surveys often take longer than expected, especially with young learners who may need items read aloud.",
"For older audiences, a call to action might be a specific next step such as downloading a reputable app, scheduling a screening or sharing information with family. Make the action concrete and achievable.",
chk('Which is the best close for a fifth-grade drink lesson?',['A quick recap game, a drink-swap pledge and a post-quiz','Ending abruptly when time runs out','Reading the references aloud','Starting a new topic'],'Summarize, commit and evaluate.')]],
['Team roles and backup plans',[
'Assign each member clear roles: lead presenters for each segment, a timekeeper, materials manager, photographer (with permission) and helpers during activities. Teams of up to six can rotate presenting so everyone contributes; Round Two asks you to explain each member’s role.',
'Plan **backups**: a non-technology version of any video or slide activity, extra materials, a shorter version if time is cut and a bonus activity if you finish early.',
'Rehearse the lesson before teaching it, ideally with a few people from the audience’s age group.',
"If possible, pilot one activity with a small group of younger HOSA members or siblings. Even a short trial reveals unclear directions and helps the team estimate time more accurately.",
"Write a brief script or talking points for each segment, especially the hook and close, but avoid memorizing word for word. Natural, conversational delivery keeps learners engaged.",
order('Preparing to teach',['Assign roles and segments','Prepare materials and backups','Rehearse with timing','Revise activities that run long','Confirm room and technology with the host'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Timing problems',{a:{text:'In rehearsal, your 30-minute lesson takes 42 minutes. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Shorten or cut segments that do not directly teach the objectives','b','Correct.',true],['Talk faster on teaching day','b','Rushing hurts learning.',false]]},b:{text:'On teaching day, the projector fails. What do you do?',choices:[['Switch to printed label cards and a whiteboard backup','c','Correct.',true],['Cancel the lesson','c','Backups keep the lesson going.',false]]},c:{text:'Your lesson runs on time with a strong opening and close.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Structure lessons with an opening hook, a body that teaches and practices key messages, a check for understanding and a close; models such as 5E (Engage, Explore, Explain, Elaborate, Evaluate) help.','Use active learning—demonstrations with practice, games, role-plays, scenarios, think-pair-share and product creation—changing activities about every 5–10 minutes and tying each to objectives.','Write a timed outline with time, segment, presenter and materials; close with a summary, a call to action and evaluation; assign roles, plan backups and rehearse.'],terms:[['Hook','An opening activity that captures learners’ attention.'],['Active learning','Strategies in which learners do, discuss or practice.'],['Timed outline','A schedule of lesson segments with times, presenters and materials.']],mcqs:[q('What is the purpose of a hook?','Capture attention at the start','Fill time at the end','Replace objectives','Evaluate learning','Opening.'),q('Which is the first step of the 5E model?','Engage','Evaluate','Explain','Elaborate','Sequence.'),q('About how often should activities change for young learners?','Every 5–10 minutes','Every hour','Never','Every 30 seconds','Attention.'),q('Which is an active learning strategy?','Role-play','Reading slides aloud','Silent lecture','Watching the clock','Doing.'),q('A timed outline lists:','Time, segment, presenter and materials','Only names','Only references','Only photos','Format.'),q('Which belongs in a strong close?','Summary, call to action and evaluation','A new topic','Silence','An apology','Close.'),q('Why plan backups?','To keep teaching if technology or timing fails','To confuse the audience','To add pages','They are not useful','Preparation.'),q('About how much of the lesson should the opening take?','About 10%','About 50%','About 90%','None','Short.')],frq:{prompt:'Design a timed outline for a 30-minute health lesson and explain how it engages learners from opening to close.',rubric:[['Structure',['opening','hook','body','check','close','5e']],['Active learning',['demonstration','practice','game','role-play','discussion','activity']],['Timed outline',['time','segment','presenter','materials','minutes']],['Close and roles',['summary','call to action','pledge','post-quiz','roles','backup','rehearse']]],model:'For a 30-minute lesson on hands-only CPR for ninth graders, our timed outline would follow the 5E model. From 0:00 to 3:00, one member would hook the class with a question—“If someone collapsed in the cafeteria right now, what would you do?”—and a quick hands-up poll, then introduce the team and the goal. From 3:00 to 5:00, another member would give a short pre-quiz. From 5:00 to 12:00, two members would explain and demonstrate the two steps of hands-only CPR—call 911 and push hard and fast in the center of the chest—using a manikin and a song with the right tempo. From 12:00 to 22:00, students would rotate through practice stations in small groups while all team members coached, followed by a quick scenario in which each group decides what to do when a classmate collapses. From 22:00 to 26:00, students would play a rapid true-or-false review game. From 26:00 to 30:00, the final member would summarize the key messages, invite students to sign a “Ready to Help” pledge card and give the post-quiz. Each segment lists the time, presenter and materials and connects to an objective. One member would be timekeeper and another would manage materials; we would prepare a backup video-free demonstration and a shorter practice rotation in case time runs short, and we would rehearse with friends before teaching.'},sources:[RULES,BLOOM]};
